Package: SCARP
Type: Package
Title: Single-Cell Chemoresistance Assessment via Rank-Based Projection
Version: 1.0
Authors@R: c(person("Linxin", "Xie", email = "xielinxin0330@gmail.com",
    role = c("aut", "cre")))
Description: We developed a rank-based cumulative scoring algorithm, termed
    SCARP, to quantify chemotherapy resistance at single-cell resolution in
    colorectal cancer (CRC). Gene-specific log fold-change (logFC) values were
    derived from differential expression analysis between chemotherapy
    non-response (NR) and response (R) samples in a CRC chemotherapy-response
    dataset and were incorporated into SCARP as a reference chemoresistance
    signature. Under the NR-versus-R contrast, positive logFC values represent
    genes preferentially expressed in non-response samples, whereas negative
    logFC values represent genes preferentially expressed in response samples.
    .
    For each cell, genes were ranked according to their normalized expression
    levels, and the corresponding gene-specific logFC values were cumulatively
    summed along the ranked gene list. The cumulative deviation with the largest
    absolute magnitude was defined as the observed score (MaxScore). To account
    for expression-dependent background variation, TopN genes were randomly
    sampled without replacement from the top 50% of matched genes ranked by mean
    expression for 3,000 iterations. Sampled genes retained their
    mean-expression rank order. The resulting empirical distribution was used to
    estimate the background mean (Random_mean) and standard deviation
    (Random_sd).
    .
    The observed score was standardized against this background and further
    adjusted using a position weight (Pw), which emphasizes deviations arising
    from highly ranked genes, and a direction strength term (Ds), which captures
    the dominance of the non-response- or response-associated cumulative
    trajectory. The final single-cell chemoresistance score was calculated as
    (MaxScore - Random_mean) / Random_sd * Pw * Ds. The score measures signed
    deviation relative to the empirical background. Higher scores indicate
    greater association with the non-response signature relative to that
    background. Its sign need not equal the sign of the unstandardized
    cumulative deviation.
